            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary consensus interprets 'gnashing of teeth' as the extreme anguish, despair, and remorse of the wicked suffering in eternal conscious torment or eschatological punishment. Alternative views identify it as hostile rage or temporal despair preceding annihilation.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the phrase as the rage and hatred of the wicked directed at the righteous upon seeing the kingdom they forfeited, which immediately precedes their destruction ('melt away').\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Anguish, sorrow, and remorse of the condemned in eternal eschatological punishment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Hostile rage and hatred of the wicked against the righteous, culminating in annihilation.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"While both apply the term to the condemned at final judgment, the book rejects the primary consensus view of eternal conscious anguish and remorse, instead identifying it as rage preceding destruction.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The primary consensus views the gnashing as an expression of sorrow and suffering in unending torment. The book asserts it signifies hostile rage and hatred that concludes with the wicked melting away or perishing, explicitly opposing the 'commonly taught' view of everlasting torment.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book adopts alternative readings recognized by the consensus (defiant rage and annihilationism) and elevates them to the core definition, directly contradicting the mainstream primary meaning of remorseful anguish in eternal conscious hell.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The phrase in the Synoptic Gospels is heavily paired with 'weeping' and 'outer darkness' or 'fiery furnace', which traditionally and contextually emphasizes profound suffering and sorrow over exclusion rather than purely hostile rage.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
